WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:08.000
So yesterday was the first day of the second&nbsp;
impeachment trial of Donald Trump, and we we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:15.040
some hints that it was going to be an absolutely&nbsp;
embarrassing, pathetic attempt at an impeachment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.040
defense. And one of the reasons we thought that&nbsp;
was that multiple times in legal briefs submitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.480
by Donald Trump's various beleaguered legal teams,&nbsp;
they misspelled the United States. They misspelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:35.440
it as United States. And that probably was not&nbsp;
the auspicious start that they were going for.&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:44.240
And it ended up being an absolutely farcical&nbsp;
presentation. Yesterday was a day it it was day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.400
one. But at the same time, it wasn't in the sense&nbsp;
that the topic of four hours of debate yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.920
four hours of presentations, rather, there was&nbsp;
not debate among senators. The topic, the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:02.480
for yesterday's four hours of presentations was&nbsp;
merely to convince the Senate one way or the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:09.520
about whether the Senate has the authority and&nbsp;
the jurisdiction to hold an impeachment trial of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.880
a president that is no longer president, a former&nbsp;
president, a past president, and why you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.640
have the Senate decide about constitutionality? I&nbsp;
don't know. And many legal scholars were not clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.600
as to why the Senate was being put in the position&nbsp;
of having to decide whether it was constitutional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.600
But that's what yesterday was about.
The Senate decided not by a huge margin, 56 to 44,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.840
that, yes, the Senate can hold an&nbsp;
impeachment trial of a president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.120
who is no longer the sitting president.
Democratic Congressman Jamie Raskin opened the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.280
proceedings as one of the impeachment managers.&nbsp;
He spoke really well included in his presentation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.320
an absolutely devastating video of what happened&nbsp;
on January 6th, cutting back and forth from what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.520
Donald Trump was saying and what was happening&nbsp;
at the Capitol. And then it was the turn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:09.520
It was Trump's lawyers turn and a guy named Bruce&nbsp;
Castor spoke. We covered it live and he droned on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.120
for about 50 minutes about nothing germane to&nbsp;
the impeachment of Donald Trump. Now, you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.200
say that must be an exaggeration, David. It must&nbsp;
have been somewhat related to the impeachment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.280
I couldn't figure out how our live audience of&nbsp;
about 40000 people couldn't figure out how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.960
was related to Bruce Castor going on about&nbsp;
British law before the Revolutionary War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.840
his personal relationships with&nbsp;
some of the senators in the chamber.&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.360
It's absurd. It's unintelligible. Let's&nbsp;
look at some clips here as Bruce Castor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.480
starting with his own childhood and the&nbsp;
political preferences of his parents.&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.560
When I was growing up in suburban Philadelphia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:59.040
my parents were big fans of Senator Everett&nbsp;
Dirksen from Illinois. And Senator Dirksen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:06.000
recorded a series of lectures that&nbsp;
my parents had on a record. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.800
we still know what records are right on the&nbsp;
thing you put the needle down on and we play.&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:20.320
So that type of stuff went on about ten minutes.&nbsp;
Then Kastor started talking about how cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.720
and great senators are as individuals&nbsp;
and how senators tend to like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.520
states that they are from their families.
They love the states that they represent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:43.200
there isn't a member in this room who has not&nbsp;
used the term I represent the great state of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:50.960
fill in the blank. Why? Because they're all great.&nbsp;
Yeah, but you think yours is greater than others?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:59.520
Because these are your people. These are the&nbsp;
people that sent you here to do their work.&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:06.000
It genuinely seemed like maybe Bruce Castor&nbsp;
prepared for some other trial about something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.600
different than the impeachment of Donald&nbsp;
Trump, because none of what he said made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:18.080
any sense. It became like an open mic night,&nbsp;
which then continued with more discussion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:24.640
what it means to be in the United States&nbsp;
Senate as far as Bruce Castor was concerned.&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:31.360
So senators are Patriots, senators are family men&nbsp;
and women. They're fierce advocates for the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:38.080
state in which they represent and somewhere far&nbsp;
down that list of attributes, way below Patriot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:46.720
and way below love of family and country and way&nbsp;
below fierce advocates for their states far down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:57.920
At least that's what I thought anyway, and I still&nbsp;
think that somewhere far down that list, senators&nbsp;&nbsp;

